  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Express $z=14(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$",
  "worked_solution": "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =14(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =14(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{14}{2} \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{3}-7\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
